
The stamp as drafted trips two of your own rules, both fixable by declaring it outside the artifact boundary rather than by softening the rules.

Line 3, "One-step increase in warmth," is a register value rendered as an adjective — R4 forbids exactly that, and R6a has already dropped the sample that would normally satisfy it. And a three-line header preceding the deck reads as the artifact describing its own structure, which is U12. Both dissolve once the stamp is defined as pre-artifact apparatus: U12 measures from the artifact's first line, and the stamp's third line is a gesture ledger, not a register claim — it names axis and step count because the artifact immediately demonstrates them.

`...` also needs to go. An ellipsis reads as latency, and in a mode whose whole point is not stopping, it invites a reply.

## New container

````
<stamp>
Fires only when /fast AND artifact mode. /slow prints the full readback. /spec prints the
projected readback. Never both.

INTERPRETATION
<artifact type + function>              — always
<structural shape>                     — always
<axis + step count>                     — only if a gesture was given
Proceeding.

Hard cap 3 value lines, ≤8 words each. The stamp never grows. If three lines cannot carry the
reading, print instead: "Reading exceeds stamp capacity — /slow recommended." Then proceed on
your current reading anyway.

Stands outside the artifact boundary: U12 and D1 measure from the artifact's first line.
Exempt from R4 — the third line names axis and magnitude without a sample, because the artifact
supplies the sample as its own first paragraph or slide.

No BOUNDS TRIPPED, no ASSUMPTIONS, no QUESTIONS in the stamp. Those go in a trailer after the
artifact, budgets unchanged. /fast buys speed by moving the correction point later, not by
suppressing what needs correcting.

"Proceeding." is terminal. No ellipsis, no offer, no question.
</stamp>
````

Your draft, corrected, is what it should look like in practice — the only change being punctuation and the reading of line 3 as a ledger entry rather than a claim.

## Scale ratified, with continuation mechanics

Append to `<scale>`:

````
Continuation: "continue" resumes at the next stub in spine order. Do not reprint the spine.
Print the stub's headline, then the built section.

A correction arriving mid-build lands on the spec (R14), then invalidates every built section
downstream of the changed line. Name them in the readback response and rebuild on continuation.
Never leave text standing that the current spec no longer produces.

Continuation inherits the original track, delivery mode, and gesture ledger. A stamp does not
reprint.
````

## Sequencing the remaining three

The dependency runs 1 → 3, with 2 already substantially done: the readback container is written, and what remains is the stamp above plus R14's interaction with continuation, which is now specified. So the real order is **calibration, then assembly**, with readback closed out as part of assembly.

One finding that argues calibration can't be done on paper. D3 and D4 are expressed as percentages, and both destabilise at low slide counts. A five-content-slide deck hits 40% at two three-bullet slides — a threshold meant to catch monotony instead fires on a deck that has barely established a pattern. D4's 60% layout ceiling fails the same way: three slides sharing a layout out of five is unremarkable. Suggested fix, to be confirmed against real decks rather than assumed:

> D3, D4 — percentage thresholds activate at ≥8 content slides. Below that: D3 fails at 4+ consecutive three-bullet slides; D4 does not apply.

The budgeted universal entries have the same exposure. U6 at 1 per 500 words, U7 at 1 per 500, P4 at 2 per 1000, P5 at 1 per 150 — those numbers are estimates, and the only way to know whether they're tight or slack is to run three or four real tasks across `/markup` and `/canva` and count what a good artifact naturally produces. Calibrating against the corpus you actually want to hit is cheap. Guessing and then discovering U7 fires on every competent deck is not.

**Cost:** `<stamp>` ~130 tokens, continuation block ~70. Both fire conditionally, so typical-path cost is lower than the total suggests.
